---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import FAQ from '../components/FAQ.astro';

const site = 'https://poke-survivors.org';
const title = 'Fusion Bonds System: Combine Moves in PokéSurvivor';
const description = 'Everything you need to know about Fusion Bonds in PokéSurvivor. Defeat legendaries, fuse up to 5 moves in one slot, and build the ultimate move set.';
const updated = '2026-10-19';
const gameVersion = '2026-10-11 update';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'How many Bonds can I have at once?',
    a: 'You can have up to 4 Bonds active at once. This lets you fuse up to 5 moves into a single move slot, freeing up slots for more moves.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do Bonds carry over between runs?',
    a: 'No official word on this, but they likely reset each run like level progress. You keep PokéCoins and gacha tickets, but Bonds need to be re-earned from legendaries.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I unfuse moves after using a Bond?',
    a: 'Nobody knows—the update didn\'t say. Can\'t tell if you can cancel or reconfigure fusions once they\'re set. Try it in-game and report back.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do fused moves share cooldowns?',
    a: 'Not documented. They fire together, but whether cooldowns interact is anyone\'s guess. Test and see.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I fuse evolved moves like Fire Blast?',
    a: 'The system doesn\'t restrict move types, so evolved moves should work. Evolve your moves first, then fuse them—that way your fusion slot has max-power attacks, not base forms.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do Bonds work in co-op mode?',
    a: 'The game says rifts support group play, but doesn\'t confirm if all players get Bonds. Likely yes—co-op rewards are usually shared. Check our <a href="/co-op/">co-op guide</a> for details.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which moves should I fuse first?',
    a: 'Prioritize high-frequency, low-cooldown moves. Fast-attacking moves benefit most from fusion. Avoid fusing slow, high-damage moves that fire infrequently.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What\'s the difference between Bonds and Move Evolution?',
    a: 'They\'re independent systems. Move Evolution transforms moves when they reach max level (like Ember → Fire Blast). Bonds fuse multiple moves to fire simultaneously from one slot. See our <a href="/how-to-play/">how to play guide</a> for move evolution details.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: title,
    description,
    datePublished: '2026-10-19',
    dateModified: updated,
    author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PokeSurvivor Fan Guide' },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'PokeSurvivor Fan Guide' },
    inLanguage: 'en',
  },
  {
    '@type': 'HowTo',
    name: 'How to Use Fusion Bonds in PokéSurvivor',
    description: 'Step-by-step guide to unlocking and using the Fusion Bonds system',
    step: [
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Enter Rifts and Fight Legendaries',
        text: 'When rifts appear on the map, enter them to battle legendary Pokémon in type-themed arenas.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Defeat Legendary Pokémon',
        text: 'Win the rift battle to earn one Bond per legendary defeated.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Fuse Your Moves',
        text: 'Use Bonds to link moves together so they fire simultaneously from one slot.',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'HowToStep',
        name: 'Stack Bonds for Maximum Effect',
        text: 'Collect up to 4 Bonds to fuse 5 moves in one slot, freeing up slots to learn more moves.',
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  alternates={[
    { locale: 'en', href: '/fusion-bonds/' },
    { locale: 'zh-Hant', href: '/zh/fusion-bonds/' }
  ]}
  description={description}
  keywords="fusion bonds, pokesurvivor bonds system, move fusion, legendary rewards, 4 bonds, 5 move fusion, rift battles, fusion strategy guide, move slot management"
  ogType="article"
  ogImage="/og-fusion-bonds.jpg"
  ogImageAlt="Fusion Bonds System guide for PokéSurvivor"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
    { name: 'Fusion Bonds', url: '/fusion-bonds/' }
  ]}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <div class="page-wrapper">
    <article class="main-content">
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="section-label">Guide</p>
        <h1>Fusion Bonds System: Combine Moves in PokéSurvivor</h1>
        <p class="article-meta">
          Updated {updated} · Based on {gameVersion}
        </p>
      </header>


      <section>
        <h2>What are Fusion Bonds?</h2>

        <figure class="content-image">
          <img
            src="/images/content/fusion-diagram-visual.svg"
            alt="Fusion Bonds system showing two moves combining into one slot"
            width="800"
            height="450"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Fusion Bonds allow multiple moves to fire from a single slot simultaneously</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The October 11, 2026 update introduced Fusion Bonds—a system that lets you combine multiple moves into
          a single slot. Defeat a legendary Pokémon in a rift, earn a Bond, fuse two moves together. They fire
          at the same time from one slot instead of taking up two.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each additional legendary you beat adds another move to that fusion. Up to 4 Bonds at once, which means
          5 moves firing simultaneously from a single slot. The other slots? Free to learn more moves.
        </p>
        <p>
          Not the same as <a href="/how-to-play/">Move Evolution</a>—that's when Ember becomes Fire Blast
          at max level. Bonds don't transform moves; they link them. Two independent systems working side by side.
        </p>
        <p>
          Why introduce this? Late-game runs hit a wall: you max out your 4 move slots, and your build's locked.
          Bonds break that limit. More moves, more type coverage, more flexibility. The same update made
          <a href="/rifts/">rifts</a> appear more often, so you have more chances to earn Bonds.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>How to Get Bonds</h2>
        <p>
          One way to get Bonds: <strong>defeat legendary Pokémon inside rifts</strong>. No other method. No shop,
          no gacha, no leveling milestone. You fight a legendary, you win, you get a Bond. One Bond per legendary.
        </p>

        <h3>Rifts: The Gateway to Bonds</h3>
        <p>
          Rifts are mysterious portals that open periodically on the map. You'll see them appear a few minutes
          into your run. Enter one, and you're teleported to a type-themed arena—18 total arenas, one for each
          type. Fire legendaries fight in the Igneous Volcano (lava burns, sun boosts Fire). Ice legendaries in
          the Eternal Glacier (slippery ice, snow chips your HP). Each arena has terrain effects and weather that
          shift the battle.
        </p>
        <p>
          You have 2 minutes to win. Beat the legendary, you get a Bond plus a ×10 gacha ticket. Lose or run out
          of time, you're sent back to the map at 30% HP with nothing. The run continues, but you walk away empty-handed.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our <a href="/rifts/">rifts guide</a> covers all 18 arenas, terrain hazards, and which partner types
          work best in each. If you're serious about collecting Bonds, read that first.
        </p>

        <h3>87 Legendary Pokémon</h3>
        <p>
          The game has 87 legendary Pokémon from generations 1 through 9—Articuno, Mewtwo, Rayquaza, Zacian, all of them.
          Dual-type legendaries (like Dialga: Steel/Dragon) randomly pick one of their types to decide the arena.
          You won't know which arena until you enter.
        </p>
        <p>
          You can tackle rifts solo or with friends. In co-op, everyone enters the rift together. If you win,
          each player should receive a Bond (the official description doesn't confirm this, but co-op rewards are
          typically shared—check our <a href="/co-op/">co-op guide</a> for multiplayer details).
        </p>

        <h3>When Rifts Appear</h3>
        <p>
          Rifts open randomly, but the October 11 update increased their spawn rate. You'll usually see your first
          rift between minutes 1 and 5. After that, they appear more frequently if you keep clearing them. Miss
          one, and another will spawn—no penalty for skipping, but every rift you skip is a Bond you don't get.
        </p>
        <p>
          Strategy: chase rifts in the mid-game (minutes 10-20) once your moves have evolved. Early-game (minutes 0-10),
          focus on leveling up and evolving your core moves first. Late-game (minutes 20-30), you're preparing for
          <a href="/arceus-judgment/">Arceus Judgment</a>, so grab any rifts you see, but don't die trying.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Fusion Mechanics Explained</h2>

        <figure class="content-image">
          <img
            src="/images/content/fusion-synergy-calculation.svg"
            alt="Fusion synergy bonus calculation showing how move combinations create damage multipliers"
            width="800"
            height="450"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>Synergy bonuses stack when moves share types or complementary effects</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3>How Fusion Works</h3>
        <p>
          The first Bond links two moves. They fire at the same time from a single slot. Example: you fuse Thunderbolt
          and Ice Beam. When Thunderbolt's attack triggers, Ice Beam triggers too. One slot, two attacks, simultaneous fire.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second Bond adds a third move to that fusion. Now it's Thunderbolt + Ice Beam + Flamethrower, all from
          one slot. The third Bond adds a fourth move. The fourth Bond adds a fifth. That's the cap: 4 Bonds, 5 moves
          in one slot.
        </p>
        <p>
          Official wording: "Every legendary you defeat in a rift gives you a bond: link two moves so they fire at
          the same time from a single slot, and each further legendary adds another one, up to 4 at once."
        </p>

        <div class="fusion-diagram">
          <div class="fusion-step">
            <strong>1 Bond:</strong> 2 moves → 1 slot <span class="free-slots">(frees 1 slot)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="fusion-step">
            <strong>2 Bonds:</strong> 3 moves → 1 slot <span class="free-slots">(frees 2 slots)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="fusion-step">
            <strong>3 Bonds:</strong> 4 moves → 1 slot <span class="free-slots">(frees 3 slots)</span>
          </div>
          <div class="fusion-step highlight">
            <strong>4 Bonds:</strong> 5 moves → 1 slot <span class="free-slots">(frees 4 slots!)</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="disclaimer-box">
          ⚠️ <strong>Mechanism Clarification:</strong> The "up to 4 at once" interpretation (5 moves in 1 slot) is
          based on the official description "each further legendary adds another one." If you observe different behavior
          in-game, the community is still exploring this system—share your findings!
        </div>

        <h3>What This Means for Your Build</h3>

        <figure class="content-image">
          <img
            src="/images/content/fusion-tier-system.svg"
            alt="Fusion combination tier list from S-tier to C-tier showing best move synergies"
            width="800"
            height="500"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>S-tier fusions combine type coverage with status effects for maximum efficiency</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Normally, you have 4 move slots. That's 4 moves total. With 4 Bonds, you fuse 5 moves into 1 slot.
          That leaves 3 slots open. 5 fused moves + 3 empty slots = room for 3 more moves. You're running 8 moves
          instead of 4.
        </p>
        <p>
          Or you run 6 or 7 moves and save slots for upgrades. The point: Bonds free up space. More moves means
          better type coverage, more utility options, more flexibility when the run throws curveballs.
        </p>

        <h3>Important Limits and Unknowns</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Maximum 4 Bonds</strong> — you can't stack beyond 4 Bonds, so 5 moves in one slot is the cap.</li>
          <li><strong>Single fusion group</strong> — the official description suggests all bonded moves fuse into
            one slot, not 4 separate 2-move pairs. Different from having 4 independent fusions.</li>
          <li><strong>Can you cancel a fusion?</strong> — unknown. The update doesn't say. Test in-game.</li>
          <li><strong>Can you reconfigure which moves are fused?</strong> — unknown. Assume it's locked once set.</li>
          <li><strong>Do fused moves trigger on the same cooldown?</strong> — unclear. They fire together, but
            each move likely keeps its own cooldown timer. Needs testing.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Fusion vs. Move Evolution</h3>
        <p>
          Bonds and Move Evolution are independent. Evolution happens when you max out a move and draw its specific
          upgrade card (Ember → Fire Blast, Bubble → Surf). Bonds fuse existing moves without changing them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Can you fuse evolved moves? The system doesn't restrict it, so Fire Blast + Surf + Psychic should work.
          Evolve your moves first, then fuse them. That way, your fusion slot has max-power attacks
          instead of base forms.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Fusion Strategies</h2>

        <div class="disclaimer-box">
          💡 <strong>Community Strategies:</strong> The following fusion strategies are based on community experience
          and game mechanics analysis, not official guides. Best practices may evolve as players discover optimal builds.
        </div>

        <h3>What to Fuse</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Prioritize high-frequency moves.</strong> Fusion doubles your output only if the moves actually fire.
          Fast-attacking moves like Thunderbolt, Water Gun, or Quick Attack benefit the most. They trigger often, so
          combining them multiplies your DPS.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Avoid slow moves.</strong> High-damage moves with long cooldowns (Hyper Beam, Giga Impact) don't
          gain much from fusion. If a move fires once every 10 seconds, fusing it with a 2-second-cooldown move
          wastes the fusion potential. The fast move fires 5 times while the slow move fires once. Better to keep
          them separate.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Cover multiple types.</strong> Fuse moves from different types to handle varied enemies. Water +
          Electric + Grass + Fire gives you answers to Rock, Ground, Fire, Water, Grass, and Ice types. Type coverage
          matters more in long runs where you face every type of horde.
        </p>

        <h3>Fusion Timing</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Evolve first, fuse second.</strong> Don't rush to fuse your starter moves. Wait until they evolve.
          A fusion of Ember + Bubble is weaker than Fire Blast + Surf. Get your moves to their final forms before
          linking them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Mid-game is the sweet spot.</strong> Minutes 10-20 is when your core moves have evolved, and you
          start seeing rifts more often. That's your window to collect Bonds and set up your fusion. By minute 20,
          aim to have at least 2 Bonds active.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Don't force it early.</strong> If a rift spawns at minute 2 and you're still working on your first
          evolution, skip it. Dying to a legendary at minute 2 ends your run. Rifts spawn frequently—another one will come.
        </p>

        <h3>Build Archetypes</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Offensive builds:</strong> Fuse 4-5 high-DPS moves (Thunderbolt, Ice Beam, Flamethrower, Shadow Ball,
          Psychic). Use your free slots for utility moves like healing or shields. This maximizes damage output while
          keeping survival options available.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Balanced builds:</strong> Fuse 3 offensive moves, leave 1 slot for a defensive move. The fusion
          slot handles damage, the other slots handle positioning, healing, or crowd control. Works well for players
          who prefer safety over raw offense.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Specialist builds:</strong> Fuse moves that all hit the same weakness. Example: all Water-type moves
          to demolish Fire/Rock/Ground enemies. Risky if you run into Grass or Electric types, but devastating in the
          right matchup. Our <a href="/tier-list/">tier list</a> shows which Pokémon excel at type-focused builds.
        </p>

        <h3>Common Mistakes</h3>
        <p>
          <strong>Fusing low-damage moves.</strong> If your moves deal 50 damage each, fusing them doesn't suddenly
          make them strong. Fusion multiplies frequency, not power. Fuse moves that are already strong.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Ignoring type effectiveness.</strong> Fusing 5 Fire moves sounds cool until you hit a Water horde.
          You'll deal 30% damage while they shred you. Spread your types unless you're running a niche build.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Skipping rifts to "save time."</strong> Bonds are worth the 2-minute investment. Every Bond you
          skip is a slot you can't free up. If you're strong enough to clear the map, you're strong enough to
          fight a legendary.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>When to Hunt for Bonds</h2>

        <h3>Early Game (Minutes 0-10)</h3>
        <p>
          Focus on leveling up and evolving your starter moves. If a rift spawns and you're confident you can win,
          take it. If not, skip it. Your first priority is getting Ember to Fire Blast or Bubble to Surf. Bonds
          come second.
        </p>

        <h3>Mid Game (Minutes 10-20)</h3>
        <p>
          This is your Bond farming window. Your moves have evolved, your stats are solid, you can handle legendary
          battles. Actively chase rifts during this phase. Aim to collect 2-3 Bonds before minute 20.
        </p>
        <p>
          Don't tunnel vision on Bonds, though. If you're low on HP or surrounded by enemies, heal up first. A
          dead run earns zero Bonds.
        </p>

        <h3>Late Game (Minutes 20-30)</h3>
        <p>
          You're preparing for <a href="/arceus-judgment/">Arceus Judgment at minute 30</a>. If rifts spawn, clear
          them—Bonds help in Judgment mode. But don't go out of your way to hunt them. Survival comes first. If
          you die chasing a rift at minute 28, the Bonds don't matter.
        </p>
        <p>
          By minute 25, shift your focus to defensive upgrades: HP, speed, shields. Bonds are a bonus at this point,
          not the goal.
        </p>

        <h3>Post-Judgment (Minutes 30+)</h3>
        <p>
          If you're still alive after minute 30, you're in Judgment mode. Rifts still spawn, but the pressure is
          intense—Arceus is chasing you, legendaries spawn every 5 seconds. If you spot a rift and have breathing
          room, grab it. The extra Bond could be the difference between minute 31 and minute 32.
        </p>
        <p>
          But don't die for it. Judgment mode survival is about not making mistakes, and risky rift dives are mistakes.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Advanced Tips</h2>

        <div class="disclaimer-box">
          🔬 <strong>Advanced Mechanics:</strong> Some of these tips involve unconfirmed mechanics (like cooldown
          interactions or upgrade card effects on fused moves). Test them in your own runs to verify.
        </div>

        <h3>Co-op Bond Farming</h3>
        <p>
          Playing with friends? Rifts are easier in groups. More players means more damage, more crowd control, more
          room to revive downed teammates. If one goes down, the others finish the legendary.
        </p>
        <p>
          Nobody's confirmed if all players get Bonds when the group wins. Based on how co-op rewards work (everyone
          gets coins and tickets), all players should get Bonds. Test it. Check our <a href="/co-op/">co-op guide</a>
          for full multiplayer strategies.
        </p>

        <h3>Arena Selection Strategy</h3>
        <p>
          You can't choose which arena you enter—rifts are random—but you can prepare. If your partner is Water-type,
          Fire and Ground arenas are favorable. If you're Electric, avoid Ground arenas (you deal 30% damage).
          Our <a href="/rifts/">rifts guide</a> lists all 18 arenas with type matchups and terrain hazards.
        </p>
        <p>
          Dual-type legendaries pick one of their types randomly. Dialga (Steel/Dragon) could send you to the Steel
          Fortress or Dragon Sanctuary. You won't know until you enter. Bring a balanced move set to handle surprises.
        </p>

        <h3>Move Slot Management</h3>
        <p>
          With 4 Bonds, you fuse 5 moves into 1 slot. That leaves 3 slots. How you fill them depends on your playstyle:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>3 more offense moves</strong> — 8 moves total, maximum damage output. Glass cannon approach.</li>
          <li><strong>2 offense, 1 utility</strong> — 7 moves total, with a heal or shield for safety.</li>
          <li><strong>2 offense, 1 open slot</strong> — 6 moves total, leaving room for situational upgrades or
            experimental moves.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          There's no "correct" split. Long runs favor utility. Short, aggressive runs favor pure offense. Adjust
          based on how the run's going.
        </p>

        <h3>Fusion and Upgrades</h3>
        <p>
          When you level up and draw an upgrade card, it affects one move. If that move is in your fusion slot,
          does the upgrade apply to all 5 fused moves or just the one? The update doesn't say. Early player reports
          are mixed. Test it yourself: fuse 2 moves, upgrade one, watch how it behaves.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our hypothesis: upgrades apply per move, not per slot. A +20% damage upgrade on Thunderbolt boosts
          Thunderbolt, not Ice Beam. But no confirmation yet. Community's still figuring this out.
        </p>
      </section>

      <FAQ heading="Common Questions" items={faq} />

      <aside class="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-title">
        <h2 id="sources-title">Sources and Updates</h2>
        <p>
          This guide is based on the <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/news/" rel="noopener">official October 11, 2026
          update announcement</a>, the <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/legendaries/" rel="noopener">legendaries page</a>,
          the <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/guide/" rel="noopener">official guide</a>, and community play experience.
          We're fans who play PokéSurvivor, not the developers.
        </p>
        <p>
          The Fusion Bonds system is new (introduced October 11, 2026), so mechanics are still being explored. If any
          information on this page conflicts with the game, trust the game. Always check
          <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/" rel="noopener">pokesurvivor.com</a> for the latest updates.
        </p>
        <p class="version-info">
          <strong>Game version:</strong> October 11, 2026 update<br>
          <strong>Guide last updated:</strong> October 19, 2026
        </p>
      </aside>
    </article>

    <section class="closing-cta" aria-labelledby="closing-cta-title">
      <h2 id="closing-cta-title">Ready to Stack Some Bonds?</h2>
      <p>Defeat legendaries, collect Bonds, and build the ultimate move set.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href="https://pokesurvivor.com/en/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn btn-primary btn-lg">Play PokéSurvivor</a>
        <a href="/rifts/" class="btn btn-secondary btn-lg">Read the Rifts Guide</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-wrapper {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 100%;
  }

  .main-content {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-2xl) var(--container-padding);
  }

  .article-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .section-label {
